<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Imad al-Din Zengi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1085–1146</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Imad al-Din Zengi was a Turkish atabeg who became governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a> in 1127 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> in 1128, founding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zengid-dynasty">Zengid dynasty</a>. He spent years trying to take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> from its Burid rulers, the Begteginid dynasty, and in 1144 captured Edessa, destroying the first of the crusader states and prompting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-crusade">Second Crusade</a>. He was murdered by a slave in 1146 while besieging <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qalat-jabar">Qal'at Ja'bar</a>; his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nur-al-din">Nur al-Din</a> continued his work.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/imad-al-din-zengi.jpg" alt="Imad al-Din Zengi" width="320" height="509" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author, 1237 CE · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ruler_in_Turkic_dress_(long_braids,_fur_hat,_boots,_fitting_coat),_in_the_Maqamat_of_al-Hariri,_1237_CE,_probably_Baghdad.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Muslim world the kingdom’s presence provoked a revival of the ideal of jihad under <b>Zengi</b>, Nur al-Din and Saladin, which helped unite Syria and Egypt.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>On Christmas Eve 1144, after a siege begun on 28 November, <b>Imad al-Din Zengi</b>, atabeg of Mosul and Aleppo and founder of the Zengid dynasty, took Edessa, the capital of the first of the crusader states, which Baldwin had founded in 1098.</blockquote><a href="/essays/christmas-day-in-history">Christmas Day in History</a></li><li><blockquote>After a siege begun on 28 November, <b>Imad al-Din Zengi</b>, atabeg of Mosul and Aleppo, took Edessa, capital of the crusader County of Edessa, the first of the crusader states.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-24">24 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Zayn al-Din Ali Kuchuk, son of Begtegin, was a Turkmen officer of <b>Imad al-Din Zengi</b> who became governor of Erbil and other towns around 1144.</blockquote><a href="/state/begteginid-dynasty">Begteginid dynasty</a></li><li><blockquote>To resist <b>Zengi</b> of Aleppo, the regent Mu'in al-Din Unur allied with Jerusalem, but the Second Crusade nonetheless besieged Damascus in July 1148 and withdrew after five days.</blockquote><a href="/state/burid-emirate-of-damascus">Burid Emirate of Damascus</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Imad al-Din Zengi</b> took Edessa on 24 December 1144 while Joscelin II was absent, provoking the Second Crusade.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-edessa">County of Edessa</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/syria">Syria</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qalat-jabar">Qal'at Ja'bar</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/syria">Syria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=36.2,37.16&amp;year=1124" aria-label="Aleppo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5486 857 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Imad al-Din Zengi"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5936 1127h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5936 1127h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=36.2,37.16&amp;year=1124">What was here in 1124? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
